L000408	\vspace{-0.4cm}
L000409	\subsection{Giant graviton integrated correlator at large-$N$}
L000410	\vspace{-0.2cm}
L000411	
L000412	We now derive the large-$N$ expansion of the integrated correlator at fixed coupling $\tau$. As discussed, $g^{(2)}_N(s)$ in \eqref{eq:gN(s)} is exponentially suppressed and we will omit its contribution in this limit.
L000413	Hence we shall focus our attention solely towards $g^{(1)}_N(s)$. Using \eqref{eq:fN}-\eqref{eq:hN} we compute the large-$N$ expansions for the building blocks
L000414	\begin{align}
L000415	f_N(s) &\label{eq:largeNspec1}= \sum^{\infty}_{\ell=0} N^{1-s-\ell} f^{(\ell)}(s) +(s\to1-s)\,,\\
L000416	h_N(s) &\label{eq:largeNspec2}= \sum^{\infty}_{\ell=0} N^{1-s-\ell} h^{(\ell)}(s) +(s\to1-s)\, ,
L000417	\end{align}
L000418	where we find
L000419	\begin{align}
L000420	   f^{(\ell)}(s) &\label{eq:fell}=
L000421	  \frac{ \tan (\pi  s) \, \Gamma (s-1)^2 }{2^{2s+\left\lfloor \frac{\ell}{2}\right\rfloor }\sqrt{\pi }\, \Gamma \left(s {+} \left\lfloor \frac{\ell}{2}\right\rfloor {+} \frac{1}{2}\right)} P^{(\ell)}_1(s)\,,\\
L000422	    h^{(\ell)}(s)
L000423	    &\label{eq:hell} =
L000424	   \frac{ \Gamma(s-1)}{(1-2s)} P^{(\ell)}_2(s) \, ,
L000425	\end{align}
L000426	with $P_1^{(\ell)}(s),P_2^{(\ell)}(s)$ simple polynomials in $s$, e.g.
L000427	\begin{align}
L000428	P_1^{(0)}(s) &\label{eq:P1}= 1\,,\qquad P_1^{(1)}(s) = \frac{1-s}{2}\,,\\
L000429	P_2^{(0)}(s) &\label{eq:P2}=1\,,\qquad P_2^{(1)}(s) = \frac{ (s-1) (s-2)}{2}\,.
L000430	\end{align}
L000431	Note that since the spectral integral \eqref{eq:spectral} and the Eisenstein series \eqref{Eisen_series_definitions} are invariant under $s\leftrightarrow 1-s$, we will omit the reflected part $(s\to 1-s)$ in \eqref{eq:largeNspec1}-\eqref{eq:largeNspec2} and multiply the terms presented by a factor of $2$.
L000432	
L000433	
L000434	Substituting the large-$N$ expansions \eqref{eq:largeNspec1}-\eqref{eq:largeNspec2} in \eqref{eq:spectral}, we evaluate the spectral integral by closing the contour of integration towards $\Re s\to +\infty$ while collecting residues using the expressions \eqref{eq:fell}-\eqref{eq:hell}.
L000435	We obtain in this way the modular invariant large-$N$ expansion for the giant graviton integrated correlator,
L000436	\begin{equation}
L000437	\mathcal{C}_\mathcal{D}(\tau;N) \sim \widetilde{{C}}(N) -\widehat{E}(1; \tau) - \sum_{\ell=1}^{\infty} N^{{1 \over 2}-\ell} \, \mathcal{C}_{\cD}^{(\ell)}(\tau)\, . \label{eq:genusMod}
L000438	\end{equation}
L000439	Some further comments on the derivation of the above result are in order.
L000440	Firstly, at any given order in large-$N$ we find that the spectral overlap has finitely many poles located at half-integer values of $s$.
L000441	This leads to expansion coefficients which are linear combinations of Eisenstein series with half-integer indices,
L000442	\begin{align} \label{eq:lDD}
L000443	\mathcal{C}_{\cD}^{(\ell)}(\tau) = \sum_{k=1}^{\ell} c^{(\ell)}_{k}\, E\big(k+ \tfrac{1}{2}; \tau \big) \, ,
L000444	\end{align}
L000445	where
L000446	$ E(s;\tau) = 2E^*(s;\tau)/\Gamma(s) $,
L000447	with $E^*(s;\tau)$ given in \eqref{Eisen_series_definitions}.
L000448	The coefficients $c^{(\ell)}_{k}$ are rational numbers, with examples given in
L000449	\eqref{eq:clk}.
L000450	
L000451	The residue at $s=1$ has to be treated carefully. For $\ell=0$, we find a double pole at $s=1$ which leads to the term $\hat{E}(1;\tau)$ in \eqref{eq:genusMod}, which is a regularised version of the singular Eisenstein series $E(1;\tau)$, i.e.
L000452	\begin{align} \label{eq:E1}
L000453	&\notag \hat{E}(1;\tau) = \lim_{s\to 1} \left[E(s;\tau) - \frac{1}{s-1}\right] \\
L000454	&= \frac{\pi \tau_2}{3} +2\gamma -\log(4\pi \tau_2)
L000455	 + 2\sum_{k\neq 0}  e^{2\pi i k\tau_1}\sum_{m|k} {1 \over m}\,,
L000456	\end{align}
L000457	where $\gamma$ is Euler-Mascheroni constant. For $\ell\neq 0$, the residue at $s=1$ yields a coupling-independent constant
L000458	which, when combined with $C(N)$ in \eqref{eq:spectral},
L000459	leads to the large-$N$ expansion of the coupling-independent part,
L000460	\begin{align}
L000461	\!\! \widetilde{{C}}(N) =  2N {-} \log(N) - 2 +\! \sum_{k=1}^{\infty}  {2 \over N^k } \!  \left[\frac{B_k}{2k}- (-1)^k\right] \, ,
L000462	\end{align}
L000463	 where $B_k$ denotes the $k$-th Bernoulli number.
L000464	
L000465	Our results are extremely important also from an holographic point of view. At large-$N$ and fixed $\tau$, since $1/\sqrt{N} \sim \alpha'$ we see that each term appearing in \eqref{eq:genusMod} corresponds to stringy corrections to two-graviton scattering in the presence of D3-branes in AdS$_5 \times S^5$. In particular, the functions $\hat{E}(1;\tau)$ and ${E}(\tfrac{3}{2};\tau)$ are associated with the BPS higher-derivative terms $R^2, D^2R^2$, and are in agreement with expectations~\cite{Bachas:1999um, Basu:2008gt, Lin:2015ixa}.  The leading term $2N$ of the coupling independent part $\widetilde{{C}}(N)$  reproduces the tree-level supergravity contribution~\cite{Chen:2025yxg}, while the subleading terms warrant further holographic study.
L000466	
L000467	We stress that the spectral integral \eqref{eq:spectral} receives additional modular invariant contributions from the large-$s$ region, which are however exponentially suppressed in $N$~\cite{Dorigoni:2024dhy}. We find two different types of such non-perturbative corrections, which at leading order are
L000468	\begin{equation}\label{eq:NP}
L000469	\mathcal{C}_\mathcal{D}(\tau;N)\vert_{\rm NP} {=}\! -2 N^{\tfrac{3}{4}} \pi^{\frac{1}{2}} D_{\frac{N}{4}}(\tfrac{1}{4};\tau) {\pm }i 4 N^{\frac{1}{2}}  D_N(\tfrac{1}{2};\tau)+...\,.
L000470	\end{equation}
L000471	The real-analytic modular function $D_N(s;\tau)$ first introduced in \cite{Dorigoni:2022cua} (see also \cite{Luo:2022tqy}) is defined as
L000472	\begin{equation}\label{eq:DN}
L000473	D_N(s;\tau) = \!\! \! \sum_{(n,m)\neq (0,0)} \!\!\!  e^{-4\sqrt{N \pi \frac{|n\tau+m|^2}{\tau_2}}} \frac{\tau_2^s/(16\pi)^s}{ |n\tau+m|^{2s}}\,.
L000474	\end{equation}
L000475	The non-perturbative terms in \eqref{eq:NP} proportional to $D_{N/4}(s;\tau)$ give rise to the leading corrections, and they originate from the large-$s$ asymptotic of the spectral integral for $h_N(s)$ as in \eqref{eq:hell}. The sub-leading corrections in \eqref{eq:NP} proportional to $D_{N}(s;\tau)$ instead come from $f_N(s)$ as in \eqref{eq:fell}.
L000476	In Appendix \ref{sec:Hooft} we give higher-order corrections to \eqref{eq:NP}. The seeming $\pm i$ ambiguity in \eqref{eq:NP} reflects the non-Borel summability of the asymptotic expansion \eqref{eq:genusMod}, and the non-perturbative corrections \eqref{eq:NP} are precisely the ambiguity-cancelling contributions required for a well-defined resurgent completion~\cite{Dorigoni:2024dhy}.
L000477	
L000478	
L000479	The usual ’t Hooft large-$N$ expansion at fixed $\lambda$ is readily extracted from \eqref{eq:genusMod} by using $\tau_2 = 4 \pi  N/\lambda$ and keeping only the zero mode of the non-holomorphic Eisenstein series \eqref{Eisen_series_definitions}.
L000480	Explicitly, in the large-$N$ 't Hooft regime the giant graviton integrated correlator is written as
L000481	\begin{equation}
L000482	\mathcal{C}_{\mathcal{D}}(\tau; N) \sim\mathcal{I}(\lambda) =  \sum_{\ell=0}^\infty N^{1-\ell} \mathcal{I}^{(\ell)}(\lambda)\,,
L000483	\end{equation}
L000484	where the genus-$\ell$ contribution is given by
L000485	\begin{equation} \label{eq:Ilambda}
L000486	\! \mathcal{I}^{(\ell)}(\lambda)= \int_{\mathcal{C}}  \frac{{\rm d} s}{\pi i} \, \big[f^{(\ell)}(s) + h^{(\ell)}(s) \big] \, \xi(2s)\, \left( \frac{\lambda}{4\pi^2} \right)^{-s}  .
L000487	\end{equation}
L000488	The contour of integration $\mathcal{C}$ is chosen as to separate the poles located at $-s\in \mathbb{N}^{\geq 1}$, whose contributions correspond to the weak coupling expansion, from all remaining poles, located at $s=0$ and $s = k+1/2$ with $k\in \mathbb{Z}$ and $k \geq -\lfloor \frac{\ell}{2}\rfloor$, which instead contribute to the strong coupling expansion.
L000489	The expression \eqref{eq:Ilambda} provides the 't Hooft large-$N$ expansion of the giant graviton integrated correlator to all orders. In particular, we reproduce the first two orders obtained in \cite{Brown:2024tru}, namely terms with $\ell=0, 1$. In Appendix~\ref{sec:Hooft} we present several new higher-order terms.
L000490	
L000491	Importantly, from \eqref{eq:hell} we note that the integrand of \eqref{eq:Ilambda} proportional to $h^{(\ell)}(s)$ is analytic for ${\Re}(s)>2$. Hence for strong 't Hooft coupling, the corresponding contribution to the Mellin representation \eqref{eq:Ilambda} will produce only a finite number of $1/\lambda$ corrections and it will be otherwise responsible for the leading non-perturbative corrections of the form $O(e^{ - \sqrt{\lambda}})$.
L000492	These corrections have been studied at genus-zero in \cite{Brown:2025huy} for a more general class of giant graviton correlators.
L000493	
L000494	 Such non-perturbative effects arise precisely by considering the 't Hooft limit of the modular invariant functions $D_{N/4}(s;\tau)$ appearing in \eqref{eq:NP}.  The integrand of \eqref{eq:Ilambda} proportional to $f^{(\ell)}(s)$ is instead responsible for smaller non-perturbative corrections of the form $O(e^{ - 2\sqrt{\lambda}})$ which coincide with the 't Hooft limit of the modular invariant functions $D_{N}(s;\tau)$ appearing in \eqref{eq:NP}. Holographically, these contributions appear to be releated to $(p, q)$-string world-sheet instantons~\cite{Dorigoni:2022cua}.
L000495	\vspace{-0.46cm}
L000496	\subsection{Exact solution for the U$(N)$ giant graviton integrated correlator}
L000497	\vspace{-0.2cm}
L000498	
L000499	We now consider the  $\mathrm{U}(N)$ theory. Unlike what we found for the SU$(N)$ theory, the U$(N)$ giant graviton integrated correlator does not receive $O(N^{-N})$-type contributions. This absence is what allows us to obtain an exact closed-form solution valid for all $N$ and $\tau$. Following the same procedure carried out for SU$(N$),  we derive a spectral representation identical to \eqref{eq:spectral}, with spectral overlap given by
L000500	\begin{align}\label{eq:UNg}
L000501	 \tilde{g}_N(s) =
L000502	f_N(s)
L000503	+ \frac{\pi \big[ \binom{N+1-s}{1-s} - \binom{N+s}{s} \big]}{(2s{-}1)\, \sin(\pi s)} \, ,
L000504	\end{align}
L000505	where $f_N(s)$ was given in \eqref{eq:fN} and we note that the second factor is again symmetric in $s\leftrightarrow 1-s$.
L000506	This simple expression provides the complete result for the giant graviton integrated correlator in the U$(N)$ theory, and we have explicitly verified the expression perturbatively and at the one-instanton level.
L000507	
L000508	The large-$N$ expansion for the second contribution to \eqref{eq:UNg} is easy to derive,
L000509	\begin{align}
L000510	\frac{\pi  \binom{N+1-s}{1-s}}{(2s{-}1)\, \sin(\pi s)} &\label{eq:largeNUN} = \sum^{\infty}_{\ell=0} N^{1-s-\ell}
L000511	   \frac{ \Gamma(s-1)}{(1-2s)} \tilde{P}^{(\ell)}(s) \, ,
L000512	\end{align}
L000513	and it is precisely of the same form as that of $h_N(s)$ given in \eqref{eq:largeNspec2}-\eqref{eq:hell}.
L000514	The polynomials $\tilde{P}^{(\ell)}(s)$ with $\ell=0,1$ in fact coincide with the corresponding SU$(N)$ results \eqref{eq:P2}, and furthermore, just as for the expansion of $h_N(s)$ in SU$(N)$, we note that at large-$N$ each order of \eqref{eq:largeNUN} is analytic for ${\rm Re}(s)>1$.
L000515	As explained below \eqref{eq:genusMod}, from the large-$N$ expansion of \eqref{eq:UNg} we evaluate the analogous spectral overlap integral \eqref{eq:spectral} for the U$(N)$ giant graviton correlator by closing the $s$-contour of integration to ${\rm Re}(s)\to +\infty$.
L000516	As a consequence of these facts, we find that at large-$N$ the leading and sub-leading orders of the SU$(N)$ and U$(N)$ theories are identical, in agreement with the results of~\cite{Brown:2024tru} in the large-$N$ 't Hooft expansion.  At higher orders, the polynomials $\tilde{P}^{(\ell)}(s)$ generally differ between the two theories; however, these differences  affect only the coupling-independent constants and exponentially suppressed terms.
L000517	
L000518	
L000519	In terms of 't Hooft expansions, this means that at any given genus the SU$(N)$ and U$(N)$ giant graviton integrated correlators are identical to all orders in the strong-coupling expansion, up to $\lambda$-independent constants. This suggests that certain coupling-dependent dynamics of giant graviton correlators are insensitive to the difference between SU$(N)$ and U$(N)$ gauge groups, and constitutes another universal property of giant graviton integrated correlators, analogous to those uncovered in~\cite{Brown:2025huy, DeLillo:2025stg}.
L000520	
L000521	\vspace{-0.6cm}
L000522	\subsection{Conclusion and discussion}
L000523	\vspace{-0.3cm}
L000524	
L000525	In this work, we obtain exact solutions in the Yang-Mills coupling $\tau$ for the giant graviton integrated correlator in $\cN=4$ SYM, to all orders in the large-$N$ expansion for gauge group SU$(N)$ and for arbitrary $N$ in U$(N)$. Despite the formidable complexity of the underlying computation, the result takes a remarkably simple form, making SL$(2,\mathbb{Z})$
L000526	invariance manifest.
L000527	Thanks to our results, we determine at weak coupling the giant graviton HHLL correlator to the two-loop order for arbitrary $N$.
L000528	We also reveal that the coupling-dependent part of the large-$N$ strong 't Hooft coupling expansion is universal between SU$(N)$ and U$(N)$ theories to all orders. Holographically, our large-$N$ analysis at finite $\tau$ provide exact constraints on two-graviton/D3-brane scattering in AdS$_5 \times S^5$ at finite string coupling.
L000529	
L000530	Our results open up several interesting directions for future work. An immediate question is to determine the $O(N^{-N})$ contributions in the SU$(N)$ case and, perhaps more importantly, to understand their holographic interpretation. Another direction is to extend our analysis to sub-determinant operators and dual giant gravitons for which presently only the leading large-$N$ ’t Hooft expansion is known \cite{Brown:2025huy}, and the associated modular functions have yet to be understood. Moreover, our all-orders results make it possible to investigate whether the universality between the giant graviton integrated correlators here discussed and those in certain $\mathcal{N}=2$ SCFTs~\cite{DeLillo:2025stg, Chester:2025ssu}, currently established at the first two orders at large $N$, extends to higher orders. Finally, it would be interesting to generalise our results to other gauge groups, where the action of S-duality on the correlators is even more nontrivial \cite{Goddard:1976qe}. In these cases, analogous lattice-sum representations have been identified only for integrated correlators of light operators \cite{Dorigoni:2022zcr, Dorigoni:2023ezg}, and it would be valuable to extend this analysis to include giant gravitons.
